<article class="definition-page">
  <header class="archival-header">
    <h1 lang="grc">λύπη</h1>
    <p class="phonetic">/ˈly.pɛː/ · lu-pe</p>
  </header>

  <section class="etymology">
    <p>
      In Koine Greek, <em lang="grc">λύπη</em> is usually translated pain, grief, or sorrow. Here it is understood as a <strong>lens</strong>, a way of looking at the world.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      Via lupe: by way of grief, observing reality through the lens of grief.
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Grief is not only a destination or an obstacle; it is a way of seeing. To look <em>via lupe</em> is to admit that grief can sharpen or blur what we perceive, and that what we notice through it deserves to be remembered, saved, and shared.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="marginalia-note">
    <h2>Notes on <span lang="grc" class="lowercase">λύπη</span></h2>

    <p>
      Greek writers set <span lang="grc">λύπη</span> against <span lang="grc">ἡδονή</span>, pleasure or delight. It could name bodily pain, from hunger or cold or sickness, and also the sorrow of misfortune and death, or the sting of insult.
    </p>

    <p>
      The <em>Theological Dictionary of the New Testament</em> notes that joy and sorrow are woven through the whole of human life, and that even the happiest things, marriage and children, bring their share of grief. It puts it plainly: <q>there is no <span lang="grc">ἡδονή</span> without <span lang="grc">λύπη</span>.</q> Sorrow, too, raises the question of why, and in doing so opens the world to us.
    </p>

    <p>
      In the New Testament the word carries that same double edge. Paul writes that <q>godly grief produces a repentance that leads to salvation</q> (2 Corinthians 7:10), and Jesus promises his friends that <q>your grief will turn into joy</q> (John 16:20).
    </p>

    <p class="source-note">See <em>Theological Dictionary of the New Testament</em>, s.v. <span lang="grc">λύπη</span>.</p>
  </section>

